WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.197
Lecture 7 built the linked list.

00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:04.967
This lecture uses it.

00:00:05.047 --> 00:00:07.300
Two applications show why we

00:00:07.381 --> 00:00:09.231
want linked structures

00:00:09.231 --> 00:00:11.886
polynomials and sparse matrices

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:14.461
where most of the data is zero.

00:00:14.542 --> 00:00:17.116
And three classic exercises show

00:00:17.197 --> 00:00:19.369
how to think with pointers.

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:21.220
First, a quick review.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.403
First, a review of the four kinds of

00:00:24.475 --> 00:00:26.210
list from lecture 7.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.801
A singly linked list

00:00:27.873 --> 00:00:30.115
each node holds one element and

00:00:30.187 --> 00:00:32.284
a reference to the next node

00:00:32.284 --> 00:00:34.236
and the last next is null.

00:00:34.236 --> 00:00:37.201
The slides draw each node at an address

00:00:37.273 --> 00:00:39.297
and write the address of the

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.816
next node inside it.

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:43.274
That is what a Java reference is

00:00:43.346 --> 00:00:45.009
a value that says where

00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:46.600
another object lives.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.083
A doubly linked list also has prev

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:52.500
so you can walk both ways.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.350
The first prev and the last next are null.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.412
A circular list is singly linked

00:00:59.494 --> 00:01:01.877
but the last next points back

00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:04.260
to the head instead of null.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.853
And a doubly circular list

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.838
is circular both ways

00:01:08.838 --> 00:01:11.168
the last next is the head

00:01:11.168 --> 00:01:14.447
and the head's prev is the last node

00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:16.690
at address 300.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.322
A circular list has no null

00:01:19.398 --> 00:01:21.222
where a walk could stop.

00:01:21.298 --> 00:01:24.186
So a traversal stops when it gets back

00:01:24.262 --> 00:01:26.390
to the node it started from.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.986
Start p at the head.

00:01:27.986 --> 00:01:31.406
A do-while loop fits: it prints 7 first

00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:33.078
and tests afterwards.

00:01:33.078 --> 00:01:36.194
p moves to 8, which is not the head

00:01:36.194 --> 00:01:37.790
so the loop goes on.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:38.550
Then 10.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.439
Now p is back at the head

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:43.392
and the test fails.

00:01:43.392 --> 00:01:47.102
The program prints 7, 8, 10

00:01:47.102 --> 00:01:48.860
back at the head.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.958
A word of warning.

00:01:50.958 --> 00:01:54.668
A while loop that tests first never starts

00:01:54.668 --> 00:01:57.197
because p starts at the head.

00:01:57.197 --> 00:02:00.907
And a loop that waits for null never stops.

00:02:00.907 --> 00:02:04.702
With circular lists, decide where you start

00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:07.400
and test for back at the start.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.310
Why a list rather than an array?

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.310
Reading the k-th element

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.230
is instant in an array

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:15.990
but a walk in a list.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.910
Inserting after a known

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.670
node shifts an array

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.710
but changes two references in a list.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.670
And a list has one node per element.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.790
This lecture uses those last two rows.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.843
Now polynomials.

00:02:30.935 --> 00:02:34.066
The obvious way to store one is an

00:02:34.158 --> 00:02:36.920
array indexed by the exponent

00:02:37.012 --> 00:02:40.511
coeffArray[i] holds the coefficient

00:02:40.603 --> 00:02:41.892
of x^i.

00:02:41.892 --> 00:02:45.390
For 4x^3 + 2x + 1

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.508
slot 3 holds 4

00:02:47.508 --> 00:02:51.375
and highPower records the degree, 3.

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:53.677
And 3x^2 + 5

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:55.610
has degree 2.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.242
Adding is one pass over the exponents.

00:02:59.242 --> 00:03:01.401
First, clear the result.

00:03:01.401 --> 00:03:04.596
Its degree is the larger of the two.

00:03:04.596 --> 00:03:06.755
Then, from the top down

00:03:06.755 --> 00:03:10.209
add the two coefficients of x^i.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:13.059
4 + 0, 0 + 3

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:16.600
2 + 0, and 1 + 5 is 6.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.882
But clearing the result

00:03:18.966 --> 00:03:20.814
loops over every slot

00:03:20.814 --> 00:03:23.502
all 3001 of them

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:26.778
so even adding costs O(MAX_DEGREE).

00:03:26.778 --> 00:03:30.390
The array always pays for its full length.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.489
Multiplying uses a·x^i

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.168
* b·x^j

00:03:35.168 --> 00:03:37.686
= ab·x^(i+j).

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.625
Every term of the first polynomial

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:43.311
meets every term of the second

00:03:43.311 --> 00:03:46.669
and the product lands in slot i + j.

00:03:46.669 --> 00:03:50.363
2x * 3x^2 is 6x^3

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:52.546
so it goes to slot 3.

00:03:52.546 --> 00:03:55.988
Several pairs can land in the same slot

00:03:55.988 --> 00:03:59.010
which is why the code adds into it.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.223
The demo prints both polynomials, their sum

00:04:03.223 --> 00:04:06.691
and their product: 12x^5

00:04:06.691 --> 00:04:08.755
+ 26x^3

00:04:08.755 --> 00:04:12.470
+ 3x^2 + 10x + 5.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:13.829
Your turn.

00:04:13.829 --> 00:04:15.697
In this multiplication

00:04:15.697 --> 00:04:19.200
how many times does the inner statement run?

00:04:19.278 --> 00:04:21.691
How many of those runs multiply

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:23.793
2 non-zero coefficients?

00:04:23.871 --> 00:04:26.128
And which slot of the product

00:04:26.206 --> 00:04:28.308
receives 2 contributions?

00:04:28.308 --> 00:04:30.410
Pause the video and count.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.341
i runs from 0 to 3

00:04:36.435 --> 00:04:38.598
and j from 0 to 2.

00:04:38.598 --> 00:04:42.265
Row 0, row 1, row 2, all zeros

00:04:42.265 --> 00:04:45.368
and row 3: 4 times 3

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:46.590
12 runs.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.032
But P has 3 non-zero terms

00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:50.862
and Q has 2

00:04:50.862 --> 00:04:54.174
so only 6 of the 12 are useful.

00:04:54.174 --> 00:04:57.137
The other 6 multiply by a 0.

00:04:57.137 --> 00:05:00.536
And slot 3 gets 2 contributions

00:05:00.536 --> 00:05:04.284
4x^3 times 5 is 20x^3

00:05:04.371 --> 00:05:07.072
and 2x times 3x^2

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.820
is 6x^3, 26x^3 in all.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.632
The array is fine for dense polynomials

00:05:14.632 --> 00:05:17.702
where most coefficients are non-zero.

00:05:17.702 --> 00:05:20.126
Now take the slides' example.

00:05:20.126 --> 00:05:21.984
P1 has 3 terms

00:05:21.984 --> 00:05:25.054
but needs 1001 slots.

00:05:25.054 --> 00:05:26.831
P2 has 4 terms

00:05:26.912 --> 00:05:29.094
and needs

00:05:29.174 --> 00:05:30.709
1991 slots.

00:05:30.709 --> 00:05:33.456
Almost every coefficient is 0

00:05:33.456 --> 00:05:35.880
these polynomials are sparse.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.618
Multiply them with the array code

00:05:38.688 --> 00:05:40.796
and the inner loop runs almost

00:05:40.866 --> 00:05:42.130
2 million times.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.291
Only 12 of those multiplications matter

00:05:45.291 --> 00:05:48.452
most of the time is spent multiplying zeros.

00:05:48.452 --> 00:05:50.910
And memory is wasted the same way.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.546
So store only the terms that exist.

00:05:54.546 --> 00:05:58.125
Each node holds one term: a coefficient

00:05:58.125 --> 00:06:01.181
an exponent, and a next reference.

00:06:01.181 --> 00:06:03.538
P1 becomes 3 nodes

00:06:03.538 --> 00:06:07.118
10 and 1000, 5 and 14

00:06:07.118 --> 00:06:08.340
1 and 0.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.610
P2 becomes 4 nodes.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.916
The nodes are kept sorted by exponent

00:06:13.916 --> 00:06:15.082
highest first.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.416
Now the space is proportional

00:06:17.493 --> 00:06:20.760
to the number of terms, not to the degree.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.679
Because both lists are sorted by exponent

00:06:24.679 --> 00:06:28.255
adding them is the merge step of merge sort

00:06:28.255 --> 00:06:29.766
from lecture 4.

00:06:29.845 --> 00:06:32.627
Walk both lists at once and compare

00:06:32.707 --> 00:06:35.250
the exponents at the two fronts.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.396
If p's exponent is larger

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.701
copy p's term and advance p.

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.132
If q's is larger

00:06:41.132 --> 00:06:43.437
copy q's term and advance q.

00:06:43.437 --> 00:06:46.696
If they are equal, add the coefficients

00:06:46.696 --> 00:06:50.114
keep the term only if the sum is not 0

00:06:50.114 --> 00:06:52.260
and advance in both lists.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.116
The result is built at its tail.

00:06:55.194 --> 00:06:57.779
A dummy node at the front saves a

00:06:57.857 --> 00:07:00.363
special case for the first term

00:07:00.363 --> 00:07:02.634
we always append after tail

00:07:02.634 --> 00:07:05.845
and at the end we return dummy.next.

00:07:05.845 --> 00:07:07.724
When one list runs out

00:07:07.724 --> 00:07:11.170
a second loop copies the rest of the other.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:14.368
Let's trace P1 + P2.

00:07:14.368 --> 00:07:17.256
p and q start at the two heads.

00:07:17.256 --> 00:07:20.416
The result is just the dummy node

00:07:20.416 --> 00:07:21.950
with tail on it.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:26.170
1990 is larger than 1000

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.160
so copy q's term, and q moves on.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.853
1000 against 1492

00:07:32.853 --> 00:07:34.700
copy q's term again.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.050
1000 against 1: copy p's term

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.750
and now p moves.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.850
14 against 1: p again.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.016
0 against 1: copy 11x.

00:07:47.016 --> 00:07:50.184
0 against 0: equal exponents

00:07:50.184 --> 00:07:52.384
so add the coefficients

00:07:52.384 --> 00:07:54.320
1 + 5 is 6

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.488
and both pointers move on, to null.

00:07:57.488 --> 00:08:00.392
Six comparisons for seven terms.

00:08:00.392 --> 00:08:04.264
Each comparison advances at least one list

00:08:04.264 --> 00:08:07.520
so adding takes O(m + n) time.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.106
Two details make the merge correct.

00:08:11.106 --> 00:08:13.853
Add x + 1 and x - 1.

00:08:13.853 --> 00:08:15.914
The x terms give 2x.

00:08:15.914 --> 00:08:19.692
The constant terms give 1 + (-1)

00:08:19.692 --> 00:08:20.980
which is 0.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.415
The test c != 0 skips that term

00:08:24.415 --> 00:08:26.476
so no node is appended.

00:08:26.476 --> 00:08:29.396
Then tail.next is still null

00:08:29.396 --> 00:08:32.143
and tail must stay where it is

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:34.548
that is why tail only moves

00:08:34.633 --> 00:08:36.780
when a node was appended.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:41.865
So the sum is 2x, not 2x + 0.

00:08:41.865 --> 00:08:46.945
And the product prints x² - 1

00:08:46.945 --> 00:08:49.370
with no 0x term.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.529
Multiplying Q by one term, c·x^e

00:08:53.619 --> 00:08:56.571
multiplies every coefficient by c

00:08:56.661 --> 00:08:59.255
and adds e to every exponent

00:08:59.255 --> 00:09:02.924
so each partial product is still sorted.

00:09:02.924 --> 00:09:06.055
10x^1000 times Q

00:09:06.055 --> 00:09:09.098
5x^14 times Q

00:09:09.098 --> 00:09:10.619
and 1 times Q.

00:09:10.708 --> 00:09:13.482
The method multiply merges them

00:09:13.571 --> 00:09:17.150
into the result one at a time, with add.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.198
12 term products

00:09:19.198 --> 00:09:21.624
all with different exponents

00:09:21.624 --> 00:09:24.049
the product has 12 terms.

00:09:24.049 --> 00:09:25.666
A few short merges

00:09:25.666 --> 00:09:28.900
instead of 2 million multiplications.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:31.211
How much does it cost?

00:09:31.211 --> 00:09:34.358
The result can grow to m times n terms

00:09:34.437 --> 00:09:37.270
and merging the i-th partial product

00:09:37.348 --> 00:09:39.709
into it costs about i times n

00:09:39.787 --> 00:09:42.620
so the m merges cost O(m²n)

00:09:42.699 --> 00:09:44.272
in the worst case.

00:09:44.272 --> 00:09:46.082
For P1 times P2

00:09:46.082 --> 00:09:48.206
that is a few dozen steps

00:09:48.206 --> 00:09:50.960
against 2 million for the array.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:52.273
One pitfall.

00:09:52.273 --> 00:09:54.840
Add must not modify its inputs.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.846
Our version copies every

00:09:56.926 --> 00:09:58.530
term into new nodes.

00:09:58.611 --> 00:10:02.221
A version that re-links the input nodes would

00:10:02.301 --> 00:10:05.670
destroy Q the first time multiply uses it.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.376
And if you merge the partial products

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.657
in pairs, as merge sort does

00:10:11.657 --> 00:10:14.601
the cost drops to O(mn log m).

00:10:14.601 --> 00:10:16.620
That is exercise 7.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.034
To sum up.

00:10:18.034 --> 00:10:21.705
One: a linked list stores only what exists.

00:10:21.788 --> 00:10:24.375
A sparse polynomial in an array

00:10:24.458 --> 00:10:26.377
wastes memory on zeros

00:10:26.377 --> 00:10:28.879
and time on multiplying them.

00:10:28.879 --> 00:10:32.300
Two: with term lists sorted by exponent

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:35.887
adding is a merge, in O(m + n) time

00:10:35.887 --> 00:10:38.556
and a zero sum is never stored.

00:10:38.556 --> 00:10:39.140
Three

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.394
multiplying merges m partial products

00:10:42.394 --> 00:10:45.480
and add must leave its inputs alone.

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:48.113
Try this: write evaluate

00:10:48.113 --> 00:10:52.223
which returns the value of a term list at x.

00:10:52.223 --> 00:10:55.694
For 4x^3 + 2x + 1

00:10:55.694 --> 00:10:58.982
it should give 37 at 2

00:10:58.982 --> 00:11:01.630
and -5 at -1.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.936
Next, in part two: sparse matrices

00:11:04.936 --> 00:11:08.060
where the same idea saves even more.

